results.summary:
I wrote the verdict to /home/user/.wave/scaffold-main-wt/.orkestrel/veneer/engine-audit-2026-10-07/verdict.md and wrote nothing anywhere else. It is 593 lines. A sweep for banned terms over the file returns no hits after one wording fix, and every table row has the same column count as its header.

The 2026-10-07 audit pins 1028 behaviors across 13 families:
- **Reader status:** 594 have a proof, 78 are recorded departures, 185 are out of contract, and 171 are open claims.
- **Refuter rulings:** the refuters rule on 174 claims (the 171 open claims plus 2 dropdown findings and 1 shared finding outside them). They uphold 162 (31 defects, 129 gaps, and 2 nits), overturn 9, and send 3 to a probe.

The 3 probes are collapse show during a hide, the dropdown boundary string, and selector failures on toggle triggers.

The file holds:
- a per-family totals table;
- a table of every upheld claim per family, with its spec citation, engine citation, the refuter's evidence, and its severity;
- the overturned table and the 3 probes;
- 15 fix units, F1 to F15, with disjoint owned files under src/browser and tests/src/browser and an oracle case for every claim;
- 7 contract questions for the user.

The units hold 163 claim entries for 162 upheld claims, because the alert orphan-trigger claim splits between F1 (the ancestor fallback) and F3 (the error count). F1, the shared-helpers unit, carries 21 claims because every helper fix lives in src/browser/helpers.ts.

Departure rows come back from each unit as patches to guides/veneer.md, and fixture or harness edits as patches to tests/setupBrowser.ts, because both files are shared. F12 depends on the element validator that F2 adds; the other units are independent.
